        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "6280f2f6-b7f8-5056-80fd-3d5fca666371",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Tura Beach, Merimbula, New South Wales, Australia, Bega Valley Shire, Spokane, Washington, Honolulu, Hawaii\n- People: Elwood Curtin Zimmerman, Charles Montague Cooke Jr., Kenneth P. Emory, Peter H. Buck, Harold St. John, F. Raymond Fosberg, Donald Anderson, Yoshio Kondo\n- Organizations: Bernice P. Bishop Museum, University of Hawaiʻi Press, CSIRO\n- Topics: Mangarevan expedition, entomology, malacology, botany, ethnology, Australian Weevils, Insects of Hawaii, 2016 census\n\nFacts:\n- Tura Beach is a suburb of Merimbula on the South Coast of New South Wales, Australia, in the Bega Valley Shire local government area.\n- Elwood Curtin Zimmerman was born in Spokane, Washington on December 8, 1912.\n- The Mangarevan expedition was sponsored by the Bernice P. Bishop Museum of Honolulu, Hawaii and led by malacologist Charles Montague Cooke Jr.\n- The Mangarevan expedition research team included ethnologists Kenneth P. Emory and Peter H. Buck, botanists Harold St. John and F. Raymond Fosberg, malacologists Donald Anderson and Yoshio Kondo, and undergraduate entomologist E. C. Zimmerman.\n- Elwood Curtin Zimmerman was an American entomologist best known for the multivolume series Insects of Hawaii, published by the University of Hawaiʻi Press, and Australian Weevils (Coleoptera: Curculionoidea), published by CSIRO.\n- Merimbula had a population of 3,544 at the 2016 census.\n- Elwood Curtin Zimmerman died in Tura Beach, New South Wales on June 18, 2004.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
